 {:db/id "q-10485" :question/id #uuid "296c1691-3fdb-50aa-926f-7c3fb307cb51" :question/math-academy-id "q-10485" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1$ intersects the line $y=x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-10485/ma-source-field/9f26ccb9963c0e6bd54a02d27819834c749c008051900e0b3ad57f6af8296f21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
