 {:db/id "q-12997" :question/id #uuid "4870cb8f-66f0-5759-83b5-334830749092" :question/math-academy-id "q-12997" :question/problem "The equation ${a}^{2}+8a=48$ can be written in the form $(a+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+8a & =48 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a & =48 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =48 \\\\ ({a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a+{4}^{2})-16 & =48 \\\\ {(a+4)}^{2}-16 & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4{)}^{2}-16 & =48 \\\\ (a+4{)}^{2} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=64,$ and $p+q=68.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4{)}^{2} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{64} \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm 8 \\\\ a & =-4\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $a=-12$ and $a=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-12997/ma-source-field/e970bb842a259916d75d141a06867c90f7238cc05b133df66da1ffa1aad71f7f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
