 {:db/id "q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824" :answer-field/id #uuid "bdf61ccf-2627-5fa3-a051-531ba920ddff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824/ece93812d967d1ec58816d96e6d73871693d020c19dc84b6c8dd4b513b275ffc" "q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824/0a45858a305cf09d88b27c471e82d061648635fb6f2c04290610fda5f5a0ad2e" "q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824/dff6684862eb71c7b1b3e3b98f09294701a43d9ad50fc4a2dcac9d83238357bd" "q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824/e8e8c01b4c5259494051f2189be4b864c55e93e2b4aa9483952a548d0413e010" "q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824/4cda7cd783e807714ba39243be65ad5faf7b14b0d6fc1549301bd76f413138ad"] :answer-field/correct "q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824/e8e8c01b4c5259494051f2189be4b864c55e93e2b4aa9483952a548d0413e010" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-13912" :question/id #uuid "4c54e973-6e64-50b6-b096-4b16776e42ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-13912" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3(n+1)}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(x-1)^{3n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{n}^{3}(x-1)^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{3} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-1{|}^{3} & <1 \\\\ |x-1| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-1 & <1 \\\\ 0<x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(0,2).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x-1|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=0$ and $x=2$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=0$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nConsequently, the interval of convergence is $I=[0,2].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-13912/ma-source-field/c0aa44f7059b30acd1f66b0a80a3a5293582021a5cc1d9c2ac04624e3f21a824"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
