 {:db/id "q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1984d219-413d-5333-ac39-1acd7d9d0ec5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d/8661436cfd746f4b60cd5691f6ee5f8a7b3f9f18a1f8541065c1a58346bad80d" "q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d/f05e363b4806ff9170bffb969ac17a278512549bf0f5a380a1917fe4214fff42" "q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d/4836b28dd052c2d7da63abac5ad842fb7a92dc6fee04b4c2c148f88419b7c81a" "q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d/faec1baa4968c88a7613e14a58575a631b79203d9aee2bbfdbbd42fbdd23d23b" "q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d/c5564736bf65a0aea6d4e1707a2d0a41d80abe542754349572e910163901aa31"] :answer-field/correct "q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d/8661436cfd746f4b60cd5691f6ee5f8a7b3f9f18a1f8541065c1a58346bad80d" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-14050" :question/id #uuid "1270303e-0109-58be-ac65-6aecbfb8540f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14050" :question/problem "![](images/04/0470661491c0d9d44ecbbc3c87af70d4ff334f1ffd2ca56b68bd4c126f3d88de.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{7}{4}+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $7$ and $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14050/ma-source-field/5148a59791bd699980ea639858cd0bffa3600b3f1172732047cc5d892635725d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
