 {:db/id "q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "402f8b77-e682-57ca-9212-0a3e5c1ad08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b/aab1ad9ce321e6a6425295ccc726ed413e7f7f2637636c7d12fc5a2d89502fb5" "q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b/eb5a589b8fd8c810abfb0163c31d726a4360dee898585e447ee275649d40105e" "q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b/7a8a4a2a5bacf349af12b1dfa4cdf0eff32871c3c2fab8b03119314869f88d1d" "q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b/36a80e34ea956915fe307f304767a10300947e44666f1b86d04fb9e41ad06177" "q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b/cecc18c19de4b1bd11a599ed59a34f3e239ca5d95fbfc5b1cc03befe0ee83f0d"] :answer-field/correct "q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b/7a8a4a2a5bacf349af12b1dfa4cdf0eff32871c3c2fab8b03119314869f88d1d" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-14241" :question/id #uuid "0ecc876e-1e30-5d04-b02b-e0d45341e6f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-14241" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b7acbd4f24b6bbd20dd00fc042405324c069d28db0102c2cd7dd49e6b3bbccd.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,4)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-8) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(0-2) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$ and  $6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-14241/ma-source-field/8af9dbb754481b9f3422b0b7b575130b79bcfeb68748fad4598e9c211645ce4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
