 {:db/id "q-14619" :question/id #uuid "0485f61e-5d32-5461-91b2-4119de37bba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-14619" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-8x+8y+3=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-8x+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4y & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+({y}^{2}+4y) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =-\\frac{3}{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+4)+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+4) & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(y-(-2))}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,-2)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-14619/ma-source-field/33ea20d922b0d025ff84fe0ec263a1954ebef5c5eeb3f53bb5df0b5de2f38aa8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
