 {:db/id "q-15681" :question/id #uuid "128b9cef-763e-5aac-a6f0-9eae06355cd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15681" :question/problem "![](images/13/13b9598c77cd522d0d3aeee4f5abd2390822431aa49ed787b73a110113ab821c.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=25,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =25 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{25} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-9,0)$ and $(1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15681/ma-source-field/8ceaf668802de3835dd3b0de48a66b094a3645b0e2de0fb1778ef4666359b3be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
