 {:db/id "q-15684" :question/id #uuid "1d50f11f-d6f2-5605-88f1-8c45f7404cb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15684" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6d356b443128da569a6b60065769cdb56e2e0059db7b146a3c4035d18a32228.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=45,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =45 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =45 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+9 & =45 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(7,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15684/ma-source-field/7cac9ff0f15240d60752fd432e46bea7a26edf79d55c46d53f61bf511f59ac13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
