 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "dd6c82b9-14ab-538d-aa0f-037306546c3d"] :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+({y}^{2}-6y) & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =3+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+4)+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+9) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-2,3)$ and its radius is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-5144/ma-source-field/eeab2b1d30bf936155d0702c4e0a453f4ee52c0b337f41f3176a07e213fb8eba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
