 {:db/id "q-6913" :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-6913/ma-source-field/e710ece9b4531d9026b3d8479d5ec85dc1d2bb96389ba6dbff7c421116706256"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
