 {:db/id "q-7915" :question/id #uuid "17761662-c8e1-5053-be1d-f17ca1632a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-7915" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $P$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(P)=4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(P)=0,$ we conclude that $P$ is not invertible. Since $P$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $P$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $Q$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(Q)=0,$ we conclude that $Q$ is not invertible. Since $Q$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $Q$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $R$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $Q$ are singular." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-7915/ma-source-field/f7b7d0cd7d49af33309e2375604ae82586dd7813e587181cb24666ba4e7c89d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
