 {:db/id "q-8408" :question/id #uuid "216ae57a-81ed-5f5e-b145-ffbb67f1cde3" :question/math-academy-id "q-8408" :question/problem "Given that $-3-2\\text{i}=\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{2\\text{i}}$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-2\\text{i} & =\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{2\\text{i}} \\\\ 2\\text{i}(-3-2\\text{i}) & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -6\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 4-6\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n4=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4$ and $q=-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-8408/ma-source-field/c4461c031b3f6fc5ab5a378c6f3d64aea4d8c3bd3d324284778ca07689d0a9c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
