 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "fb231c9b-8ce3-5939-ba7a-76e32364f988"] :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=3$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+3)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+3)^{2}=-1.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb8a943a6f6cfd49e47c193f5379e06fd166cd17087c890233b42a7e1e7d2b89.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-8801/ma-source-field/b7d6ed069e09af0ac7dc8f57c9819b38e5515a9be7329ee74db90d21c8286fa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
