 {:db/id "q-9467" :question/id #uuid "abc24ff4-9a14-532c-930a-11e5cec7f6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-9467" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{(0+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 12+{(y-3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^{2}=-3.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-9467/ma-source-field/b533a10ae45642fef173072ea051dca560d0dd406e0eef15d1c40ea175d2cce6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
