 {:db/id "q-9913" :question/id #uuid "11af43ea-89bf-58ea-b458-4433226aa764" :question/math-academy-id "q-9913" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix  $[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{aligned}| & =k|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k(3k-6)+0+0 \\\\  & =k(3k-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(3k-6) & =0 \\\\ 3k(k-2) & =0 \\\\ k & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-9913/ma-source-field/be47fe8f22802d9a403a2ca66c8e2a00c725470849f00f6e744994a66019223f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
